      "text": {
        "title": "Die Untersuchung",
        "de": "Die Untersuchung ist am Montag. Die Krankheit ist neu. Die Operation ist wichtig. Das Medikament liegt auf dem Tisch. Das Röhrchen kommt aus dem Labor. Der Pfleger misst Fieber. Das Auge ist rot. Der Name steht in der Akte.",
        "en": "The examination is on Monday. The illness is new. The operation is important. The medication is on the table. The little tube comes from the lab. The nurse takes a temperature. The eye is red. The name is in the chart."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar",
      "type": "grammar",
      "label": "Grammar",
      "title": "Endungen",
      "practice": "choose",
      "heroTitle": "The ending often names the article",
      "heroImage": "clipboard",
      "story": [
        "In the table, <b>der</b> is blue, <b>die</b> is red and <b>das</b> is green, the same colours as the articles everywhere in MediDeutsch.",
        "Feminine endings: <b>-ung, -heit, -keit, -ion, -tät, -in</b>, and most words in <b>-e</b>. Neuter endings: <b>-chen, -lein, -ment, -um</b>. Masculine: <b>-er</b> for a person or a machine, <b>-ling, -ismus</b>, plus days and months.",
        "Three exceptions to keep: <b>das Auge</b>, <b>der Name</b>, <b>die Woche</b>. And a compound still takes the article of its last noun: die Untersuchung, but der Untersuchungsraum."
      ],
      "questionRule": "Look at the ending first. If the word is a compound, look at the <b>last</b> noun, not at -ung in the middle.",
